{"builder":"Goal: one pull request that meets both acceptance lines of issue #31:\n1. \"loadConfig(path) in src/config.js catches the JSON.parse error and returns {} instead\"\n2. \"npm test passes\"\n\nCurrent state, as read in the repository:\n- src/config.js: `loadConfig(path)` returns `JSON.parse(readFileSync(path, 'utf8'))`. A parse error is thrown to the caller.\n- src/app.js already wraps loadConfig in try/catch. It prints `bad config: <message>` to stderr and exits 1, and merges the result over DEFAULTS `{ port: 8080, greeting: 'hi' }`.\n- test/config.test.js has a test 'bad JSON throws' that asserts loadConfig throws SyntaxError on `{port: 9000`. That test contradicts line 1 and has to change.\n- README.md says an invalid JSON file stops the app with `bad config: <message>` and exit 1.\n- `npm test` runs `node --test`.\n\nSteps:\n1. In src/config.js, put the JSON.parse call inside a try/catch so that a JSON.parse failure (SyntaxError) makes loadConfig return `{}`. Catch only the parse error. Keep `readFileSync` outside the try, or rethrow anything that is not a SyntaxError, so a missing or unreadable file still throws as it does now. Valid JSON must still return the parsed object.\n2. The issue's motivation is that people can't tell which file is broken or where. Returning `{}` with no message would hide that. Inside the catch, before returning `{}`, write a one-line warning to stderr (for example with `process.stderr.write` or `console.warn`) that names `path` and includes the parse error's message. This does not conflict with line 1.\n3. Update test/config.test.js. Replace 'bad JSON throws' with a test that asserts `loadConfig(file('{port: 9000'))` deep-equals `{}`. Keep 'reads a config'. Optionally add a test that a nonexistent path still throws.\n4. With this change src/app.js no longer reaches its catch for bad JSON; the app starts with DEFAULTS. Leave app.js working; its catch still handles unreadable files. Update README.md so it describes the new behavior: on invalid JSON the app warns on stderr naming the file and starts with the defaults.\n5. Run `npm test` at the repository root and make sure it exits 0 with no failing tests.\n6. Open the pull request. In its description, say that per the issue loadConfig now returns {} on a JSON parse error, and that a stderr warning names the file and error. Also say plainly that the app now starts on defaults instead of exiting 1 on a bad config.json.","verifier":"Check the pull request head against the two acceptance lines of issue #31, one check per line, in this order. Run the commands from the repository root at the PR head.\n\nCheck 1. Expect line: \"loadConfig(path) in src/config.js catches the JSON.parse error and returns {} instead\"\nRun: write a file containing invalid JSON, then call loadConfig on it. For example:\n`printf '{port: 9000' > /tmp/bad-config.json && node --input-type=module -e \"import { loadConfig } from './src/config.js'; const r = loadConfig('/tmp/bad-config.json'); console.log('RESULT', JSON.stringify(r));\"`\nAlso read src/config.js at the head and confirm loadConfig is defined there and catches the JSON.parse error.\nPASS: the call does not throw, the printed result is `RESULT {}`, and the code in src/config.js's loadConfig catches the JSON.parse error.\nFAIL: the call throws (for example an uncaught SyntaxError), returns anything other than `{}`, or the catching is not in loadConfig in src/config.js.\n\nCheck 2. Expect line: \"npm test passes\"\nRun: `npm test` (run `npm install` first only if the repository needs dependencies).\nPASS: npm test exits successfully and reports no failing tests.\nFAIL: npm test exits non-zero or reports any failing test.\n\nAdvisory (not PASS or FAIL conditions):\n- Valid JSON should still parse. For example, `printf '{\"port\": 9000}' > /tmp/good-config.json` should give `{\"port\":9000}`.\n- A nonexistent path should still throw instead of returning {}. Only the parse error was meant to be caught.\n- The issue's motivation is telling people which file is broken. Note whether a stderr warning names the path and the parse error.\n- `node src/app.js /tmp/bad-config.json` now probably starts on defaults instead of exiting 1. Note whether README.md was updated to match.\n- The old test 'bad JSON throws' in test/config.test.js should have been replaced by one that expects {}."}